"A natural question, after two full lessons on the five zang organs and six fu organs, is: where is the brain in all of this? The honest answer is that it doesn't fit cleanly into either category, and Chinese medicine solves the problem by giving it, along with a small group of other structures, a third category entirely: the \"extraordinary organs,\" which includes the brain, bone marrow, bones, blood vessels, the uterus, and — despite being listed among the fu organs earlier — the gallbladder, which is considered to straddle both categories.": "bbdb782a23bb",
"A natural question, after two full lessons on the five zang organs and six fu organs, is: where is the brain in all of this? The honest answer is that it doesn't fit cleanly into either category, and Chinese medicine solves the problem by giving it, along with a small group of other structures, a third category entirely: the \"extraordinary organs,\" which includes the brain, bone marrow, bones, blood vessels, the uterus, and — despite being listed among the fu organs earlier — the gallbladder, which is considered to straddle both categories. The extraordinary organs share a defining feature: like the zang organs, they are solid and store something important, but unlike the zang organs, they aren't matched to one of the five elements or paired with a fu organ. The brain specifically is described as \"the sea of marrow,\" filled and nourished by the kidney's stored constitutional strength — which is why this course's earlier lesson on the kidney's role in growth and reproduction is also, indirectly, a lesson about what's believed to keep the brain properly nourished over a lifetime. This category exists because Chinese medicine's organ system was never meant to force every structure into one of two boxes. The extraordinary organs are the system's own acknowledgment that some things — the brain very much included — don't behave like either a storing zang organ or a processing fu organ, and needed their own place in the framework rather than being awkwardly squeezed into one that didn't fit.": "aee18cd1b2ae",
